- OTT Streaming Content Trend Analysis (Netflix)

1. Project Overview
This project explores streaming platform content catalogs to uncover structural patterns in genre popularity, maturity ratings, release timing trends, and content format mix over time. The workflow 
mirrors the operations of a real-world content strategy and growth acquisition team, transitioning from raw data dumps into actionable business recommendations.
---
2. Tech Stack & Tools
- Python: Pandas, NumPy, Matplotlib *(Strictly no Seaborn/Plotly)
-  SQL: MySQL (for core table creation and aggregations)
-  Excel: Microsoft Excel (Pivot Tables, PivotCharts, and Text Parsing)
---
3. Repository Deliverables

1. EDA & Visualizations:
   - Python script processing the 7-column dataset (`show_id`, `title`, `release_year`, `rating`, `duration`, `listed_in`, `type`).
   -  Generates 6  visualizations covering content mix, top genres, release trends, maturity distributions, and runtimes.

2. SQL Aggregations:
   - Relational schema creation and core SQL queries analyzing annual content growth and genre distributions.

3. Excel Workbook (`/excel/ott_analysis_pivot.xlsx`):
   - Configured with custom text-parsing formulas and interactive Pivot Tables/Charts verifying the content trends.

4. Content Strategy Memorandum:
   - Executive briefing providing 3 actionable greenlight recommendations for content acquisition leadership.
---

4. Key Insights & Recommendations
- Shift to Multi-Season Content: Reallocate 20% of indie film acquisition budgets into multi-season episodic series to improve user lifetime value (LTV).
- Regional Diversification: Balance Western-heavy catalogs by accelerating co-productions in high-growth international corridors.
- Runtime Standardization: Mandate an optimal 85–105 minute window for original feature-length commissions to maximize completion rates.
